# Static analysis, added 2026-09-08 after the third incident of the same shape.
#
# The three, in order, all of them invisible to `php -l` and to every test in
# `tests/`:
#
#   slice 5   `chain => $x` instead of `'chain' => $x`. A bareword is a
#             constant lookup, which is valid PHP and a fatal at runtime on one
#             page. `tests/Support/BarewordTest.php` now catches this one
#             specifically; an analyser catches the whole class it belongs to.
#   slice 7   `$blocked->reason` on a `Blocked` that carries a `kind` and a
#             `__toString()`. Reading an undefined property is a warning and a
#             null in PHP 8, so the blocked message was silently empty on the
#             one screen nobody had reached.
#   —         `Cause` cast to a string. It is deliberately not stringable —
#             `Newsprint\Support\Causes` exists because the library declines to
#             answer "what should a reader be told" — so the cast produced
#             nothing where a sentence belonged.
#
# What they have in common is that the program is *well-formed* and wrong, and
# that the wrong branch is one a test would have to reach to see. §8's whole
# design makes those branches rare by construction: they are the failure paths,
# and the failure paths are exactly what does not run on a good day.
#
# Level 5 rather than the maximum, deliberately. It reaches property and method
# existence, return types and argument types — which is where all three of the
# above live — without demanding the array-shape annotations that level 6 wants
# from every `array<string, mixed>` this site passes around as configuration.
# Raising it later is a decision with evidence behind it; starting there would
# be a decision with a wall of noise behind it.
parameters:
    level: 5

    paths:
        - src
        - tests
        - config
        - public/index.php
        # The `bin/` scripts have no extension, so a directory scan misses
        # them. They are named one by one, which is also a list worth having to
        # keep up to date.
        - bin/build-content
        - bin/dev-router.php
        - bin/devnet-canary
        - bin/devnet-smoke
        - bin/last-settle
        - bin/name-the-mint
        - bin/upstream-drift

    # `templates/` is not analysed and should not be. `View` renders them with
    # `extract()`, so every variable in a template is undefined as far as any
    # analyser is concerned — the reported errors would all be false and the
    # real ones would be buried among them. That gap is covered from the other
    # side: `TemplateRenderTest` renders every template in every state under a
    # strict error handler, where an undefined variable is an exception rather
    # than an empty string.

    ignoreErrors:
        # `$state?->mintDecimals ?? $default` — PHPStan is right that `??`
        # already suppresses the null-property access, so the `?->` is
        # redundant *as a matter of language semantics*. It is not redundant as
        # a matter of reading: `$state` really can be null (an unprovisioned
        # copy, or an RPC read that failed), and the `?->` is where the code
        # says so. Deleting it to satisfy the rule would leave the safety
        # resting on a subtlety of `??` that a reader has to already know.
        # Seven sites, all of that shape; none of them can hide a null
        # dereference, because the rule only fires where the access is already
        # safe.
        -
            message: '#on left side of \?\? is unnecessary#'

    # PHPDoc is documentation here, not a second type system. Where a docblock
    # and the code disagree the code wins, and an analyser that trusts the
    # comment reports a contradiction that does not exist.
    treatPhpDocTypesAsCertain: false
